{
	title: 'Terminal web links carry a one-time code',
	spec: "The host knows its public web address: a webUrl setting in config.ason (settings table), empty by default meaning http://localhost:<the port the web server really listens on> (the preferred webPort, or the next free one when another Hal holds it; links then never point at the other Hal), and every terminal holding a link code is sent the address again when the server binds, since clients connect before it does, and the terminal lays out again every row that links with the old address (the address is part of the cached rows' key, and of the reused history's), so history already in scrollback is repainted, not left pointing at the preferred port. Every web link the terminal prints points there: image markers (/image/<name>), paste markers, each tab label in the tab bar (its number links to <webUrl>/<session id>) and any later link to a block. A link's hidden OSC 8 target carries ?auth=<code>, a one-time code of task 8a; its visible text never does (codes appear in view only as the reply to /auth or ./run auth). Codes are not minted per frame: the host gives each terminal client (host terminal or peer, over its connection) a link code and replaces it once it is redeemed and before it is 5 minutes old, so a link on screen keeps working for at least 5 of a code's 10 minutes; once one click has logged a browser in, its cookie covers every later link. Link codes are never written to history, drafts or logs. A web session token lasts 10 years, as task 8a now says (it was built with 30 days). Any page the host serves (/, /<id>, /image/<name>, /paste/<name>) asked for without a valid code or cookie shows the login gate, not a bare 401, and after a right code lands on the page that was asked for. Tests: a printed link's target holds a code the host accepts and its visible text none; a used or expired code on /image/<name> gets the gate, which returns there after login; the code is replaced after redemption; no code reaches any history file.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"Protocol: the terminal sends {type: 'auth', link: true} on every connection (app.onState, after tab-start); host/web-links.ts gives that connection a code with {type: 'auth', code, link: <webUrl>} and a new one when it is redeemed (web.redeem calls webLinks.used) or after renewMs() (4.5 minutes, an unref'd timer per connection, dropped on close and in host.reset). A plain {type: 'auth'} is still the one-off ./run auth code. Reusing the auth event kept common/transcript.ts untouched: it already ignores auth events.",
		'The client keeps the address and code in ansi.state.web; ansi.webUrl(path) builds every link target, falling back to http://localhost:<webPort> until the first code arrives. Peers get the address from the host, so they need no config.',
		"Transcript rows are cached per item (frame.state.rows) and keep the code they were first painted with: putting the code in the cache key would change rows in scrollback every 4.5 minutes, and render.ts answers that with a canonical repaint that clears scrollback. So only live rows (prompt, tab bar) follow the newest code; an old row's link works for 10 minutes, then (if that browser has no cookie yet) lands on the gate. Rebuilding for width changes picks up the current code.",
		'The gate at a file address (/image/<name>, /blob/<session>/<blob>) is the page itself with status 401 and cache-control no-store (web.gate); App.tsx reloads instead of opening the chat when router.isApp says the address is not / or /<id>, so a login lands on the file. ?auth= on a file address redirects like on a page. /login and /ws still answer a bare 401.',
